    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Julius Yego - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases People World Athletics\ndate: 2012-05-03\n---\nimage_size: 250px\nnickname: Mr. YouTube\nbirth_date: 4 January 1989\nbirth_place: Cheptonon, Rift Valley Province, Kenya (today Nandi County)\nheight: 175 cm ftin on\nweight: 85 kg lb on\nsport: Track and field\nevent: Javelin throw\ncoach: Petteri Piironen Finnish advice is the secret to African javelin throwers' success Landells Steve 18 September 2014 International Association of Athletics Federations 15 September 2014 28 August 2015 live\npb: AR, CR 92.72 m (2015)\nmedaltemplates: Competition Olympic Games, Silver 2016\u00a0Rio\u00a0de\u00a0Janeiro Javelin throw, Competition World Championships, Gold 2015 Beijing Javelin throw, Competition Commonwealth Games, Gold 2014 Glasgow Javelin throw, Bronze 2022 Birmingham Javelin throw, Competition African Games, Gold 2011 Maputo Javelin throw, 2019 Rabat Javelin throw, Silver 2023 Accra Javelin throw, Competition African Championships, Gold Javelin throw, Gold 2014 Marrakech Javelin throw, Gold 2018 Asaba Javelin throw, Gold 2022 Port Louis Javelin throw, Gold 2024 Douala Javelin throw, Bronze 2010 Nairobi Javelin throw\n\nJulius Yego (born 4 January 1989) is a Kenyan track and field athlete who competes in the javelin throw. Nicknamed \"Mr. YouTube\" because he learned how to throw by watching YouTube videos of javelin athletes, Yego is the African record holder for the event with a personal best of 92.72 m.\n\nHe won the javelin title at the All-Africa Games in 2011 and at the African Championships in Athletics in 2012 and 2014; at the 2013 World Championships he placed fourth, losing a medal in the final round. In 2014, he became the first Kenyan to win a Commonwealth Games gold medal in a field event. At the 2015 World Championships he won the gold medal with a throw of 92.72m, becoming the first Kenyan to win a World Championships gold medal in a field event"
