    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Football at the Summer Olympics - Wikipedia\ndate: 2004-06-16\n---\nsize: 150\ncode: FBL\nsport: football\nmenevents: 1\nwomenevents: 1\notherlinks: Tournaments (men\u30fbwomen)\ncurrent: 1900 Summer Olympics\n\nname: Men's Olympic football tournament\nlogo: Football pictogram.svg\norganiser: IOCFIFA\nfounded: 1900The 1900 and 1904 tournaments are not recognized by FIFA. The competition has been held regularly, except 1932. Since 1992, only the U23 national teams are allowed to participate.\nregion: International\nnumber of teams: 16 (finals)(from 6 confederations)\ncurrent champions: 23 ESP(2nd title)\nmost successful team: GBRHUN(3 titles each)\ncurrent: 2024 Summer Olympics\n\nname: Women's Olympic football tournament\nlogo: Women's football pictogram.svg\norganiser: IOCFIFA\nfounded: 1996\nregion: International\nnumber of teams: 12 (finals)(from 6 confederations)\ncurrent champions: USA(5th title)\nmost successful team: USA(5 titles)\ncurrent: 2024 Summer Olympics\n\nAssociation football has been included in every Summer Olympic Games as a men's competition sport, except 1896 (the inaugural Games) and 1932 (in an attempt to promote the new FIFA World Cup tournament). Women's football was added to the official program at the Atlanta 1996 Games.\n\nIn order to avoid competition with the World Cup, FIFA have restricted participation of elite players in the men's tournament in various ways: currently, squads for the men's tournament are required to be composed of players under 23 years of age, with three permitted exceptions.\n\nBy comparison, the women's football tournament is a full senior-level international tournament, second in prestige only to the FIFA Women's World Cup.\n\nAnother major difference between the men's and women's tournaments is that the men's tournament is not included in the FIFA International Match Calendar, while the women's tournament is included. This in turn means that clubs are not required to release players for the men's tournament, but must release players for the women's event.\n\nHistory\n\nPre-World Cup era\n\nBeginnings\n\nFootball was not included in the program at the first modern Olympic Games in Athens in 1896, as international football was in its"
